tools.description:
Use in place of cat, sed, head or Read on a .scala file, and for a compiler error, a stack frame or a grep hit at `File.scala:123`: a definition's doc and signature, its body with `body`, as file text an Edit can anchor on, by name or by position. Name the member you need (`Owner.member`), and pass `body` with its name for its body, rather than showing a whole object; pass every symbol you need in one call. With a short name several definitions share, pass the full name.

To read or call a definition, by name or at a File.scala:N position: its signature and Scaladoc, with one-line outlines of the project types it names. Each definition's NN-MM header, and each outline, ends with its size, `[N.N KB]`; a class, trait, object or enum shown without its body ends with its closing lines, an anchor for an Edit adding a member to it. After them, one line per named symbol counts the tests in test sources that exercise it: `-- tests of <Sym>: N in M suites (nearest: A, B, C); …`, the line ending with a hint to list them, which over MCP is `tests` set to `list`; N the tests holding a reference to it or to a member, M the suites holding one in a test or a helper, and the nearest at most three of those suites, the ones under the symbol's own module directory first, then those holding the most references; `-- tests of <Sym>: none name it …` when no suite holds one, saying what is not counted (a call through a supertype's member it overrides, a subtype's own members), and `no test names it; helpers in M suites do` when only helpers outside every test hold one. With `tests` set to `list`, a Tests section lists them instead: a `## tests exercising <Sym>` line, then per file a `== file  package` line and entries `NN-MM <Suite>: <test name>`, or `NN-MM <Suite>: helper <def>` for a helper holding a reference itself. Those lines are not file text. A name main sources lack is looked up in test sources, under a `-- in test sources: <full name>` note, with no tests line. A short name several definitions end with picks the one outside test sources and the `secondary` paths `.outline.conf` names, when only one is, under a note naming the others; otherwise every one is shown, under an `-- ambiguous` note listing each as `find` does.

Example: {"symbols":["Ledger.post","shop/src/ledger/Posting.scala:28"],"body":["post"]}

Use in place of grep for `extends Trait`, before implementing or changing a trait: the trait and every implementation, fakes in test sources included.

To implement or change a trait: its implementations (private ones, and anonymous classes named by the definition holding them, as `Shelf.of (anonymous)`), contracts and their suites, each definition's line ending with its size, `[N.N KB]`; the trait, and with `member` its member shown without its body when that member is a type, ends with its closing lines.

Example: {"trait":"Store"}

Use in place of grep or rg for a definition's call sites and references, before changing or renaming it: every site, by file and the definition holding it, each with its line, to edit from. With a short name several definitions share, pass the full name: a short name may resolve to one of them. It takes `sites` (every site listed), `in` and `outside` (narrow by path); it has no `private` parameter.

To find who calls or names a definition before changing it. Sites are grouped by file (`== file`), then by the definition holding them: an `in <owner>` line (the member around the site, or `<Suite>: <test name>` for a site in a test) ending with the owner's size, `[N.N KB]`, then each site as `<line>  <source line>`, and last `[N sites in M files]`. Over 20 sites in all, each file prints as `== file  N sites` and each owner once as `in <owner>  N sites`, with no site lines, ending `-- N sites in M files`, with a hint to list them all by `sites`, or to narrow by `in` or `outside`. With several symbols, each site's line number is followed by `[→ <symbol>]`, the symbol asked that it is a use of, and a `-- by symbol:` line before the counts gives each symbol's sites. A `-- N references by a target's name not resolved` line counts references scout could not tie to a definition: any of them may be a use, so grep for the name when every use matters.

Example: {"symbols":["Ledger.post"]}

Use in place of grep or find to locate a definition whose name you know in part, in main or test sources: `*Name*` patterns; grep is for text that is no definition. An exact name answers whether a name a doc mentions still exists.

To find a definition when you don't know its name: one line per definition in main sources, members and constructor vals included, with its full name, file, lines, collapsed signature and size, `[N.N KB]`. Test sources' matches follow main sources', after an `-- also in test sources:` line, or when main sources hold no match, after a note saying so; when neither does, a renaming import (import a.X as Y, or a braced selector X as Y or X => Y) giving the name is named, with X's definition. A pattern whose owner is a method whose body holds the name says find lists no definition local to one, and names the show that prints its body; those notes count toward max_kb like the matches.

Example: {"pattern":"*Shelf*"}

Use in place of reading a whole .scala file, before editing its imports or adding a definition to it: its loose lines and an outline of its definitions.

To edit a file's imports or place a new definition: its loose lines (package, imports, comments outside every definition) as file text, each top-level definition and its members as `NN-MM kind name [N KB]` lines, and its last lines under `NN-MM end of file`, file text. A target several files match is NoMatch naming each; a file with no .tasty fails, naming the command that compiles it.

Example: {"target":"shop/src/ledger/Ledger.scala"}

